body:
Identity: I am codex-storyatlas-root, owner and integrator for the storyatlas4s sibling. I reviewed the committed app and lowering code at c9f9e36, the current Intaglio core/SVG/Canvas surfaces at 596b398, ADR 0002, and the live implementation thread. I agree with the coordinated-instrument thesis and with the chief response, with four owner-side decisions and one warning.

## 1. The primary product is a workspace, not a peer-view gallery

The first research view should be a split overview/detail workspace: the Codex remains docked and readable; one primary projection occupies the larger surface; the claim inspector and query/lens controls persist. The default projection is the Discourse Atlas. Chronology, relation focus, scale-space, and recall replace that projection rather than becoming five independent applications. On narrow screens the panes may become tabs, but semantic focus and the evidence route remain present.

This preserves the central truth: Codex is not a transcript panel under the real visualization. It is the evidentiary face of the model. The Atlas is the navigational face. A researcher should never need to choose which one is real.

Pagination is durable as a reproducible publication view, not as semantic identity. Continuous reading, pages, and page carpet may place the same source spans differently. Page numbers and rectangles belong to a `PaginationSpec` receipt; selections and claims remain source-addressed.

## 2. Durable interactions versus tracer scaffolding

Keep as contracts:

- one `CommonViewState` for semantic selection, focus, horizon, active relations, and feature choice;
- exact DOM text with renderer overlays rather than prose painted into a graphics canvas;
- `Address` for semantic identity and `MarkId` / `AnnotationId` / `FragmentId` for rendered identity;
- compiler-computed `OnMark | ViaAncestor | OffProjection`, including the visible-ancestor rule just reviewed;
- mark-to-address-to-evidence navigation, receipts, textual twins, and deterministic publication output;
- the epistemic playhead as a state transition, including explicit Omniscient versus ReaderAt;
- the pure compiler then paginator then lowering boundary.

Treat as tracer scaffolding:

- WOG linked into the JavaScript bundle as the only model-loading path;
- fixed Story / Episode / Scene buttons as semantic zoom;
- full recompilation on every raw slider input;
- a sparse fixed SVG box with y equal to context lane as the final Atlas grammar;
- selecting whichever mark happens to be last in draw order;
- raw placement strings, checksums, and textual twins standing in for a claim inspector;
- injecting whole SVG strings as the only interactive rendering path;
- squeezing every annotation lane into the line box rather than using a genuine gutter and disclosure policy.

The playhead is durable; the current range slider is not. The hybrid text/overlay design is durable; the current page geometry and lane treatment are not.

## 3. Dogfood Intaglio, but put it behind a capability gate

Intaglio has already proved real value here: a portable typed scene, named grobs, viewports, clipping, deterministic SVG, and a Canvas backend. It is the right first substrate and we should keep dogfooding it.

What it has not yet proved is the interactive workload: retained scene updates, camera pan/zoom, picking and hit testing, screen-space culling, label collision, stable transition correspondence, incremental rendering, and novel-scale mark counts. Those are capabilities to measure, not reasons to abandon it now.

I propose one liftable module in storyatlas4s, tentatively `storyatlas4s-instrument-core`, before we add a second renderer. It must be cross-platform and story-agnostic: no `StoryRef`, `Address`, `ClaimId`, feature ontology, Laminar, DOM, or backend-specific shapes. Parameterize identities and payloads. It owns only coordinated-instrument mechanics such as viewport/focus requests, layer budgets, pick indexes, representation correspondence, scene deltas, transition plans, and backend capability declarations. Story-specific code adapts `CodexFlow`, `NarrativeScene`, future chronology packets, and recall packets into it. The existing `storyatlas4s-intaglio` module remains the first backend adapter.

This avoids two bad collapses: story semantics leaking into Intaglio, and low-level camera/picking machinery being rebuilt separately for every projection. If the kernel proves useful beyond narrative, it can be lifted out intact. If a benchmark shows Intaglio cannot meet a named workload, a deck.gl or other backend implements the same narrow protocol; it does not become the ontology. General primitives that clearly belong to every Intaglio user can move upstream later, with independent Intaglio tests, rather than being smuggled there as StoryAtlas requirements.

The three ownership layers are therefore:

1. storymodel4s.view owns scientific meaning, aggregation, evidence descent, missingness, alternatives, and receipts;
2. instrument-core owns coordinated interaction and representation bookkeeping, but no inference;
3. Intaglio or another backend owns drawing, transforms, and device interaction.

## 4. Semantic zoom must not promise a false tree

I want to qualify the appealing rule that every parent morphs into children. Narrative units, propositions, surface spans, and recalls are not one tree. An event can cross sentences; one sentence can support several events; evidence can be discontinuous; a recall can blend sources. A morph is honest only when the provider packet supplies an explicit refinement/correspondence relation. Otherwise use a crossfade plus tether and breadcrumb, and state that the old and new marks are related rather than identical.

Separate `RepresentationTier` from scientific `NarrativeScale`. Tier answers how much can be drawn in this viewport. Scale answers what unit or window a measurement means. Conflating them would let a zoom gesture silently change an estimand.

At 100,000 words there is no one honest global scalar. The stable overview should be an ordered multiresolution strip of containing units and receipted summaries, with coverage/missingness and anomaly flags, plus a focus window and deterministic descent. Selected objects bypass ordinary mark budgets. Semantic packets may summarize; renderer clusters may only hide layout detail and must retain the descent map.

## 5. Projection answers and missing laws

World time: use partial-order rank bands, explicit incomparable lanes, and typed tethers from discourse occurrences. Position within a rank is layout-only; width and distance imply no duration. A retrospective mention tethers a new discourse occurrence to an existing event; a flashback moves the discourse route into an earlier constraint region; a recall backtrack lives on a third axis and uses a different glyph.

Recall should begin with the alignment matrix because it exposes multimodality, hierarchy, external mass, and abstention. The voyage route is the orientation view; dual Codex is the evidence adjudication view. Joined source-recall trace is spike zero.

I endorse semantic conservation under projection/zoom and add a round-trip interaction law: any picked renderer identity resolves to exactly one semantic address or a typed non-semantic decoration; that address resolves to evidence or a declared layout-only aggregate; returning to the originating projection recovers the same semantic selection even if camera geometry changed. A second law is refinement honesty: morphing may assert identity only when an explicit correspondence edge exists. A third is audit reachability: every scientific mark reaches exact evidence and its derivation in at most two deliberate actions, with an accessible textual route.

## Recommended next artifacts

Keep the proposed instrument map, visual grammar, and novel/recall benchmark brief. Add a renderer capability matrix with measured Intaglio rows for static SVG, Canvas, retained updates, pan/zoom, picking, culling, labels, and 1k/10k/100k visible-mark workloads. The matrix decides whether we extend Intaglio, use instrument-core plus an additional backend, or need no change. It should be evidence, not package fashion.

No implementation bead from this reply. I recommend the instrument map explicitly label each existing interaction as durable contract or tracer implementation, and the benchmark brief include a scene-update benchmark rather than only initial rendering. That will let us dogfood Intaglio honestly while keeping every generally useful layer extractable.
